 "notes": [
  "SIMULATED, not playtesting: the model shows wall strength and hold rates; it says nothing about whether buying or building is enjoyable.",
  "Money (PROPOSAL for the lead and the owner, under the owner's Nov 25 rule that monetization must give meaningful value): at the smallest amounts the requirement holds only for a pilot who buys the crates on most days until day 228: $5568.21 a year of wall-only ore on the ridge town ($4585.15 to $5568.21 across the seven sites; 41 ar, 71 tq, 141 se, 226 mh crates), besides the Ferrite and Ilmenite crates ($2493.47 bought at their limits every day). Every larger crate is fully built sooner for less money: m 5 {\"ar\":50,\"tq\":20,\"se\":10,\"mh\":5} fully built day 95, $2243.05; m 10 {\"ar\":100,\"tq\":40,\"se\":20,\"mh\":10} fully built day 48, $1059.04; m 30 {\"ar\":300,\"tq\":120,\"se\":60,\"mh\":30} fully built day 20, $448.51. Smaller crates mean more purchases, not a weaker paid fortress.",
  "STRONG is not the binding definition: every m measured (m 1 too) holds 100% of the late raids against the free heavy player's 81%: with the wall-only ore the paying pilot's town reaches the fully built town's ceiling (guard (a): it holds every raid at the top strength), so late raids stop being a contest for it.",
  "As needed (the lead's rule): a lower ore is bought while any ring is below its heavy blend or cells are left to build, so some is never used (at the pick: 60 ar, 6.4 tq, 11.2 se, 0 mh left on day 365); the lower crates' spend is an upper bound.",
  "Hold rates are 21 raids a day (3 a site), so one raid is 4.8 points; the requirement's 5 points is 4 more raids held of the 63 measured on days 300, 330, 365."
 ],
